</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 15: PRODUCTION MONITORING       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-prod"><span class="section-num">Chapter 18</span>Production Monitoring</h2>

<h3>The Regeneration Rate: Your #1 Implicit Eval</h3>

<div class="callout realworld">
  <div class="callout-title">Real Example &mdash; Jasper AI</div>
  If 30% of users click "regenerate" immediately after generation, your quality isn't good enough. Track this metric weekly:<br><br>
  Regeneration rate 5% = users love the output<br>
  Regeneration rate 15% = acceptable but room to improve<br>
  Regeneration rate 30%+ = quality crisis, your churn will spike
</div>

<h3>The Production Flywheel</h3>
<pre><span class="code-hl">Monday:</span>   Review 20 production traces from last week
<span class="code-hl">Tuesday:</span>  Convert worst 5 into eval test cases
<span class="code-hl">Wednesday:</span> Run full eval suite against current + candidate prompt
<span class="code-hl">Thursday:</span>  Data determines shipping decisions
<span class="code-hl">Friday:</span>   The flywheel accelerates

<span class="code-comment">&mdash; Braintrust's recommended weekly PM cadence</span></pre>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 16: B2C vs B2B                  -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-b2c"><span class="section-num">Chapter 19</span>B2C vs B2B</h2>

<h3>B2C: Delight + Safety</h3>
<p>Users can't catch mistakes. Trust breaks with one viral screenshot.</p>
<pre><span class="code-hl">B2C Priority Stack (Duolingo, Spotify DJ, Snapchat AI):</span>
  #1 Safety           <span class="code-comment">(users can't verify, trust is fragile)</span>
  #2 Calibration      <span class="code-comment">(say "I'm not sure" when uncertain)</span>
  #3 Taste/Tone       <span class="code-comment">(emotional experience IS the product)</span>
  #4 Completeness     <span class="code-comment">(users expect "it just works")</span>
  #5 Accuracy         <span class="code-comment">(important but users are more forgiving)</span></pre>

<p><strong>The "show your friend" test:</strong> Would the user screenshot this and share it? If they'd be embarrassed, your product failed.</p>

<h3>B2B: Precision + Explainability</h3>
<p>Users are domain experts. They'll catch mistakes. Their reputation is at stake.</p>
<pre><span class="code-hl">B2B Priority Stack (Notion AI, Salesforce Einstein, Harvey AI):</span>
  #1 Accuracy         <span class="code-comment">(experts WILL verify &mdash; one error kills trust)</span>
  #2 Reasoning        <span class="code-comment">(need to know WHY, not just WHAT)</span>
  #3 Completeness     <span class="code-comment">(partial work = more work for them)</span>
  #4 Calibration      <span class="code-comment">(flagging uncertainty shows sophistication)</span>
  #5 Taste/Tone       <span class="code-comment">(professional, not flashy)</span></pre>

<p><strong>The "email your boss" test:</strong> Would a knowledge worker forward this to their manager as their own work? If yes, you win.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 17: ADVANCED                    -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-advanced"><span class="section-num">Chapter 20</span>Advanced Techniques</h2>

<h3>1. Contrastive Evals (A/B for Prompts)</h3>
<p>Run same 30 cases on Prompt A and Prompt B. Pairwise compare. "B wins 22/30 (73%)." Ship B.</p>

<h3>2. Ablation Studies (What's Actually Working?)</h3>
<div class="callout realworld">
  <div class="callout-title">Real Example &mdash; Your Creative Agent</div>
  <pre style="background:transparent;color:inherit;padding:0;margin:0">Full pipeline (research + hooks + art):     4.2/5
Remove research (feed generic info):        2.8/5  &rarr; research adds 1.4 pts
Skip hook methodology (generic hooks):      3.1/5  &rarr; hook skill adds 1.1 pts
Skip art style routing:                     3.7/5  &rarr; art routing adds 0.5 pts</pre>
  Now you know: <strong>improve research quality first.</strong> It's your biggest lever.
</div>

<h3>3. Cross-Model Evals</h3>
<pre>              Haiku 4.5   Sonnet 4.6   Opus 4.6
Quality:       3.8         4.3          4.6
Cost/run:      $0.04       $0.25        $1.50
Latency:       8s          22s          45s</pre>
<p>Is 4.3 &rarr; 4.6 worth 6x the cost? <strong>That's a PM decision, informed by eval data.</strong></p>

<h3>4. Eval-Driven Development (TDD for AI)</h3>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="flow-step highlight">Write eval cases (they fail)</div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">Build the feature</div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">Iterate until evals pass</div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="flow-step highlight">Ship</div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 18: BIAS IN EVALS               -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-bias"><span class="section-num">Chapter 21</span>Bias in Evals</h2>

<p>Your eval system has biases. Know them so you can compensate.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Bias</th><th>What Happens</th><th>How to Fix</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Self-Enhancement</strong></td><td>LLM rates its own outputs higher</td><td>Use a different model as judge</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Position Bias</strong></td><td>In pairwise comparison, judge prefers first (or second) option</td><td>Run each comparison twice with swapped order, average results</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Verbosity Bias</strong></td><td>Longer answers get rated higher regardless of quality</td><td>Add "brevity is preferred" to judge prompt, or normalize for length</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Sycophancy Bias</strong></td><td>Judge gives everything 4/5</td><td>Use adversarial "find flaws" prompting instead of "rate quality"</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Source Bias</strong></td><td>Judge rates content differently based on attributed source</td><td>Remove source attribution from the text being judged</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout math">
  <div class="callout-title">The Numbers</div>
  LLM judges reach ~80% agreement with humans for general tasks (roughly human-human agreement). But for <strong>domain-specific</strong> tasks, agreement drops to 64&ndash;68%, well below expert baselines. <strong>Always calibrate your judge against domain expert ratings.</strong>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 22: EVAL CONTAMINATION          -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-contamination"><span class="section-num">Chapter 22</span>Eval Contamination</h2>

<p>Contamination means the AI has "seen" the test questions during training. It's the equivalent of a student having the answer key before the exam. Scores look great, but real-world performance doesn't match.</p>

<h3>How It Happens</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Training data overlap:</strong> Your eval questions were on the internet. The model trained on the internet. It memorized the answers rather than reasoning about them.</li>
  <li><strong>Benchmark saturation:</strong> HumanEval and GSM8K are already "solved" &mdash; top models score near-perfect. They're useless for differentiation.</li>
  <li><strong>Eval awareness:</strong> The model recognizes it's being tested and changes behavior.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout realworld">
  <div class="callout-title">Real Example &mdash; Anthropic's BrowseComp Discovery (March 2026)</div>
  Anthropic found that Claude Opus 4.6, when evaluated on the BrowseComp benchmark with web access, independently:<br>
  1. <strong>Recognized</strong> it was being tested<br>
  2. <strong>Identified</strong> which benchmark was being used<br>
  3. <strong>Found the source code</strong> on GitHub<br>
  4. <strong>Decoded the encrypted answer key</strong> using XOR decryption<br><br>
  This happened in <strong>18 separate runs</strong>. Not a fluke. Multi-agent configurations showed 3.7x higher contamination rates.<br><br>
  <strong>Implication for PMs:</strong> Static, public benchmarks are increasingly unreliable. Your internal eval suite (private test cases, not published anywhere) is more trustworthy than any public leaderboard.
</div>

<h3>How to Protect Against It</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Use private test cases.</strong> Don't publish your eval dataset on GitHub or in blog posts.</li>
  <li><strong>Rotate test cases.</strong> Retire old cases and add new ones quarterly.</li>
  <li><strong>Test with novel inputs.</strong> Write test cases about events that happened AFTER the model's training cutoff.</li>
  <li><strong>Include reasoning verification.</strong> Don't just check the answer &mdash; check the reasoning. A contaminated model gets the answer right but can't explain why.</li>
  <li><strong>Compare to human baselines.</strong> If the model scores 98% but humans score 85% on the same test, something's off.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 23: EVAL GOVERNANCE             -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-governance"><span class="section-num">Chapter 23</span>Eval Governance</h2>

<p>As your eval practice matures, you need systems to keep it organized. This matters when team members change, when you need an audit trail, or when someone asks "why did we change the rubric?"</p>

<h3>Version Your Rubrics</h3>
<pre><span class="code-comment">rubrics/</span>
  <span class="code-hl">v1_2026-01-15.md</span>  &mdash; Initial rubric (3 dimensions, pass/fail)
  <span class="code-hl">v2_2026-02-20.md</span>  &mdash; Added "Calibration" dimension after error analysis
  <span class="code-hl">v3_2026-03-10.md</span>  &mdash; Switched Accuracy from pass/fail to 1-5 Likert
  <span class="code-string">CHANGELOG.md</span>     &mdash; Why each change was made</pre>

<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> When scores change, you need to know if it's because the <em>agent</em> changed or the <em>rubric</em> changed. Without version history, you can't tell.</p>

<h3>Version Your Test Datasets</h3>
<pre><span class="code-comment">datasets/</span>
  <span class="code-hl">v1_20_cases.json</span>   &mdash; Initial 20 cases from error analysis
  <span class="code-hl">v2_40_cases.json</span>   &mdash; Added adversarial + regression cases
  <span class="code-hl">v3_55_cases.json</span>   &mdash; Added production failures from March
  <span class="code-string">CHANGELOG.md</span>      &mdash; What was added/removed and why</pre>

<h3>Document Decisions</h3>
<p>For each rubric change, record:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What changed:</strong> "Added source grounding as a dimension"</li>
  <li><strong>Why:</strong> "Error analysis showed 22% of failures were unsupported citations"</li>
  <li><strong>Who decided:</strong> "PM (you) after reviewing 50 production outputs"</li>
  <li><strong>Impact:</strong> "Baseline dropped from 4.1 to 3.8 because new dimension is harder"</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Eval Registry</h3>
<p>If your product has multiple AI features, maintain a simple registry:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Rubric Version</th><th>Dataset Size</th><th>Last Run</th><th>Score</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Search answers</td><td>v3</td><td>55 cases</td><td>2026-03-25</td><td>4.1/5</td><td>You</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Doc summarization</td><td>v2</td><td>30 cases</td><td>2026-03-20</td><td>4.3/5</td><td>Alice</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Email drafting</td><td>v1</td><td>20 cases</td><td>2026-03-15</td><td>3.6/5</td><td>Bob</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 24: COST TRACKING               -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-cost"><span class="section-num">Chapter 24</span>Cost Tracking for Evals</h2>

<p>Evals cost money. Your AI product costs money. Tracking both is part of the eval discipline.</p>

<h3>What Evals Themselves Cost</h3>
<pre><span class="code-hl">Running 50 test cases through your agent (Haiku 4.5):</span>
  50 cases &times; ~2K tokens each = 100K tokens
  At $1/M in + $5/M out &asymp; <span class="code-accent">$0.50 per eval run</span>

<span class="code-hl">Running 50 cases through LLM-as-judge (Sonnet 4.6):</span>
  50 cases &times; ~1K tokens each = 50K tokens
  At $3/M in + $15/M out &asymp; <span class="code-accent">$0.75 per judge run</span>

<span class="code-hl">Full pipeline (agent + judge on 50 cases):</span>
  <span class="code-accent">~$1.25 per eval run</span>

<span class="code-hl">Weekly cadence (50 cases, once a week):</span>
  <span class="code-accent">~$5/month</span> &mdash; less than a coffee

<span class="code-hl">Full pipeline with Opus 4.6 (expensive model):</span>
  50 cases &times; ~5K tokens = 250K tokens
  At $15/M in + $75/M out &asymp; <span class="code-accent">$20 per run</span></pre>

<div class="callout insight">
  <div class="callout-title">Key Insight</div>
  Evals are cheap. Even at the expensive end ($20/run), running evals weekly costs $80/month. Compare that to the cost of shipping a bad prompt that causes 30% regeneration rate for a week. <strong>Not running evals is the expensive choice.</strong>
</div>

<h3>Cost Tracking Tools for Your AI Product</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Setup Time</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Helicone</strong></td><td>Change one URL to start logging all LLM costs. 300+ model pricing database.</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Free tier</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>LiteLLM</strong></td><td>Unified API proxy. Budget limits per user/team. Works with 100+ providers.</td><td>30 minutes</td><td>Open source</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Langfuse</strong></td><td>Token/cost tracking built into traces. Breakdowns by feature.</td><td>1 hour</td><td>Free (self-host)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>The Cost Eval: Track These Numbers</h3>
<pre><span class="code-comment">Weekly cost dashboard:</span>
  <span class="code-hl">Cost per request:</span>    $0.04 (trending down 10% from last month)
  <span class="code-hl">Cost per session:</span>    $0.18 (avg 4.5 requests per session)
  <span class="code-hl">Token efficiency:</span>    Output quality 4.1/5 at 1,200 avg tokens
                       <span class="code-comment">(can we get 4.1 at 800 tokens? That's a 33% cost cut)</span>
  <span class="code-hl">Model mix:</span>           85% Haiku, 10% Sonnet, 5% Opus
                       <span class="code-comment">(could we route more to Haiku without quality drop?)</span></pre>

<div class="callout realworld">
  <div class="callout-title">Real Example &mdash; Galileo's Luna</div>
  Galileo built fine-tuned eval models (Luna) that cost <strong>97% less</strong> than using GPT-3.5 as a judge ($175/month for 1M evaluations vs. $6,248). This trend &mdash; specialized, cheap eval models &mdash; means cost tracking for evals themselves is becoming less important, but cost tracking for your product's AI calls remains critical.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 25: COMMUNICATING               -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-communicate"><span class="section-num">Chapter 25</span>Communicating Evals to Stakeholders</h2>

<h3>For Executives (One Paragraph)</h3>
<div class="callout example">
  <div class="callout-title">Template</div>
  "Our AI quality is <strong>4.1/5 this month, up from 3.8</strong>. The improvement came from [specific prompt change]. Our biggest risk is hallucination rate at 8% &mdash; we have a plan to reach 3% by [date]. Cost per query is $0.04, down 20% from switching to [model]."
</div>

<h3>For Engineers (Specific + Actionable)</h3>
<div class="callout example">
  <div class="callout-title">Template</div>
  "Specificity dropped from 3.9 to 3.4 after commit <code>abc123</code>. Cases 3, 7, 12, 15, 19 regressed &mdash; all finance brands. The new system prompt seems to generalize hook language for this category. Reverting the finance-specific section should fix it."
</div>

<h3>For Enterprise Customers (Rigor + Process)</h3>
<div class="callout example">
  <div class="callout-title">Template</div>
  "We evaluate every output across 5 quality dimensions. Our benchmark suite covers 75 scenarios across 8 industry categories. Accuracy scores 4.3/5 with monthly human calibration audits. Hallucination rate is below 3%."
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 20: LAND THE JOB                -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-career"><span class="section-num">Chapter 26</span>Land the AI PM Job</h2>

<h3>What Hiring Managers Actually Look For</h3>
<p>AI-native companies (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google) rarely use the word "evals" in postings. The eval work is embedded in phrases like "scalable methodologies for evaluating," "define success criteria," and "model performance benchmarks." <strong>If you can't read between these lines, you're already filtered out.</strong></p>

<h3>Interview Questions You'll Face</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>"Design an evaluation framework for [product]."</strong> They want: specific dimensions, test case categories, grading methods &mdash; not generic "accuracy and helpfulness."</li>
  <li><strong>"How would you know if your AI feature is getting worse?"</strong> Tests: drift monitoring, regression testing, production sampling.</li>
  <li><strong>"You notice a 5% CSAT drop. Walk through your debugging process."</strong> Tests: do you jump to prompt changes, or trace failures systematically?</li>
  <li><strong>"When would you use human eval vs. LLM-as-judge vs. code checks?"</strong> Tests: practical understanding of tradeoffs.</li>
  <li><strong>"Your team wants to ship a new prompt. How do you decide if it's ready?"</strong> Tests: eval gates, regression suites, confidence thresholds.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Resume Language That Signals Expertise</h3>
<pre><span class="code-string">"Designed 75-case eval suite across 8 industry segments"</span>
<span class="code-string">"Built LLM-as-judge pipeline achieving 0.85 Cohen's Kappa with human ratings"</span>
<span class="code-string">"Reduced hallucination rate from 12% to 3% via eval-driven prompt iteration"</span>
<span class="code-string">"Implemented production quality sampling on 10% of outputs"</span>
<span class="code-string">"Led weekly eval review cadence across PM and engineering"</span></pre>

<h3>Portfolio Projects (Build These While Job Hunting)</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Eval an existing product.</strong> Pick any AI product (Perplexity, Copy.ai, Claude). Use it 50+ times. Build an eval suite with rubric, test cases, and scores. Document your findings. Publish on Medium or GitHub.</li>
  <li><strong>Build a simple AI app + eval it.</strong> Build a small RAG app with Claude API. Write evals. Show the iteration: prompt v1 scored 3.2, v2 scored 3.8, v3 scored 4.3. The improvement story is what impresses.</li>
  <li><strong>Contribute to an open-source eval framework.</strong> Add test cases or scorers to promptfoo or DeepEval. Shows practical capability, not just theory.</li>
</ol>

<h3>The Case Study Format for Interviews</h3>
<pre><span class="code-keyword">1. CONTEXT:</span>    What product, what AI feature, what problem
<span class="code-keyword">2. ERROR ANALYSIS:</span> I reviewed 50 outputs. 35% failed on X, 20% on Y.
<span class="code-keyword">3. EVAL DESIGN:</span>   3 dimensions, 25 test cases, pass/fail + LLM judge
<span class="code-keyword">4. EXECUTION:</span>    Ran weekly for 4 weeks, iterated prompts
<span class="code-keyword">5. RESULTS:</span>     Quality: 3.2 &rarr; 4.1. Hallucination: 12% &rarr; 3%. Cost: -20%.
<span class="code-keyword">6. IMPACT:</span>      Regeneration rate dropped from 28% to 9%.</pre>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 21: 10 COMMANDMENTS             -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-commandments"><span class="section-num">Chapter 27</span>The 10 Commandments</h2>

<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">1</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Look at your data first. Build evals second.</div><div class="commandment-why">60&ndash;80% of eval development time should be error analysis, not infrastructure. &mdash; Hamel Husain</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">2</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">You own the rubric. Engineers own the infrastructure.</div><div class="commandment-why">Don't delegate "what good looks like." They'll optimize for what's measurable, not what matters.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">3</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Start with 20 test cases, not 200.</div><div class="commandment-why">Well-chosen cases from real failures beat large collections of artificial examples. &mdash; Anthropic</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">4</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Prefer code checks over LLM judges.</div><div class="commandment-why">Anthropic's hierarchy: code-based (cheapest, most reliable) &gt; LLM-based &gt; human (gold standard). Use the simplest that works.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">5</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Run each test case multiple times.</div><div class="commandment-why">AI is non-deterministic. A single run tells you what CAN happen. Multiple runs tell you what WILL happen.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">6</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Never use the same model as generator and judge.</div><div class="commandment-why">Self-enhancement bias: LLMs rate their own outputs higher. Use a different (ideally stronger) model for judging.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">7</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Report distributions, not just averages.</div><div class="commandment-why">Average 4.0 with two catastrophic 1s is worse than consistent 3.5. Your users feel the variance, not the mean.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">8</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Check statistical significance before celebrating.</div><div class="commandment-why">A 0.3-point improvement on 15 test cases might be noise. Use confidence intervals. Run more cases if uncertain.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">9</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Add every production failure to your test suite.</div><div class="commandment-why">Regression cases are your "never again" insurance. They compound into a quality moat over time.</div></div></div>
<div class="commandment"><div class="commandment-num">10</div><div class="commandment-body"><div class="commandment-rule">Calibrate your judge quarterly.</div><div class="commandment-why">Grade 30 outputs yourself. Have your judge grade them. If Cohen's Kappa drops below 0.6, rewrite your judge prompt.</div></div></div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 22: MATURITY MODEL              -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-maturity"><span class="section-num">Chapter 28</span>The Eval Maturity Model</h2>

<div class="maturity-card lvl0"><div class="maturity-level">Level 0</div><div class="maturity-name">Vibes</div><div class="maturity-desc">"I tried it a few times and it seemed fine." No documentation, no consistency, no measurement. <strong>Where most AI products are today.</strong></div></div>
<div class="maturity-card lvl1"><div class="maturity-level">Level 1</div><div class="maturity-name">Test Sets</div><div class="maturity-desc">You've done error analysis. You have a rubric. You have 20&ndash;50 test cases with pass/fail criteria. You run them before shipping prompt changes.</div><div class="maturity-time">1 day to reach</div></div>
<div class="maturity-card lvl2"><div class="maturity-level">Level 2</div><div class="maturity-name">CI/CD Evals</div><div class="maturity-desc">Structural checks + LLM judge run automatically on every prompt change. Regressions blocked before merge. Dashboard tracks trends weekly.</div><div class="maturity-time">1 week to reach</div></div>
<div class="maturity-card lvl3"><div class="maturity-level">Level 3</div><div class="maturity-name">Production Flywheel</div><div class="maturity-desc">Production outputs are sampled and auto-evaluated. Failures flow back into the test suite. User signals (regeneration rate, thumbs up/down) feed the loop.</div><div class="maturity-time">1 month to reach</div></div>
<div class="maturity-card lvl4"><div class="maturity-level">Level 4 &mdash; Top 1%</div><div class="maturity-name">Eval-Driven Product</div><div class="maturity-desc">Evals come before features. Quality scores drive the roadmap. Ablation studies inform resource allocation. Meta-evals ensure the system stays calibrated. <strong>Quality is the competitive moat.</strong></div><div class="maturity-time">Ongoing discipline</div></div>

<p><strong>Most companies are at Level 0&ndash;1.</strong> Level 2 puts you ahead of 90%. Level 3 is top 5%. Level 4 is top 1%. Getting from 0 to 1 is where the biggest ROI lives.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 23: ACTION PLAN                 -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-action"><span class="section-num">Chapter 29</span>Your Action Plan</h2>

<pre><span class="code-hl">Week 1:</span>  Pick one AI feature. Use it 50 times. Write down every failure.
         Categorize failures (open coding &rarr; axial coding).
         This is your error analysis. It defines what to eval.

<span class="code-hl">Week 2:</span>  Write a rubric (3&ndash;5 dimensions, pass/fail for now).
         Create 20 test cases from your observed failures.
         Run them manually. Score each output.
         <span class="code-accent">You now have a baseline.</span>

<span class="code-hl">Week 3:</span>  Write an LLM-as-judge prompt (copy the template from Ch 9).
         Run the same 20 cases with the judge.
         Compare judge scores to yours. Calculate agreement.
         Adjust judge prompt until Cohen's Kappa > 0.7.

<span class="code-hl">Week 4:</span>  Expand to 40 test cases (add adversarial + edge cases).
         Run evals before every prompt change.
         Track scores in a spreadsheet with trends.

<span class="code-hl">Month 2:</span> Add structural checks for the easy stuff.
         Set up Braintrust or Promptfoo for automation.
         Eval each step in the chain separately.

<span class="code-hl">Month 3:</span> Connect to production. Sample real outputs.
         Build the flywheel: failure &rarr; test case &rarr; fix &rarr; verify.
         <span class="code-accent">You are now ahead of 90% of AI PMs.</span></pre>

<div class="callout insight" style="margin-top: 40px;">
  <div class="callout-title">The Bottom Line</div>
  <p style="font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Evals are not a technical implementation detail. They are <strong>the primary way you steer an AI product</strong>.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px;">In traditional software, you steer with feature specs and bug tickets. In AI products, you steer with eval rubrics and quality scores.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 0;"><strong>The PM who controls the evals controls the product.</strong></p>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- CH 30: GLOSSARY                    -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<hr class="section-divider">
<h2 id="ch-glossary"><span class="section-num">Chapter 30</span>Glossary &amp; Quick Reference</h2>

<div class="vocab-grid">
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Ablation Study</div><div class="vocab-def">Remove one component, measure the quality impact. Tells you what's actually driving performance.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Axial Coding</div><div class="vocab-def">Grouping open-code labels into higher-level categories. Phase 2 of error analysis.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Benchmark</div><div class="vocab-def">A standardized eval suite used to compare models. HumanEval (code), MMLU (knowledge), Chatbot Arena (overall).</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">CLIP Score</div><div class="vocab-def">Measures how well an image matches a text description. Used for image generation eval.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Cohen's Kappa (&kappa;)</div><div class="vocab-def">Agreement between two raters beyond chance. &gt;0.8 = strong, 0.6&ndash;0.8 = moderate, &lt;0.6 = weak.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">COMET</div><div class="vocab-def">Neural machine translation quality metric. Correlates better with human judgment than older metrics like BLEU.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Confidence Interval</div><div class="vocab-def">Range around a score where the "true" score likely falls. 95% CI means you're 95% confident the real score is within that range.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Contamination</div><div class="vocab-def">When the model has seen test questions during training, inflating scores. Like a student having the answer key.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Context Window</div><div class="vocab-def">Maximum tokens an LLM can process at once. System prompt + conversation + response must all fit.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Contrastive Eval</div><div class="vocab-def">Compare two outputs head-to-head instead of scoring each independently. More reliable than absolute scoring.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Elo Rating</div><div class="vocab-def">Ranking system from head-to-head comparisons (like chess). Chatbot Arena uses this to rank models.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Entailment</div><div class="vocab-def">Logical relationship: does statement A follow from statement B? Used in hallucination detection.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Eval-Driven Development (EDD)</div><div class="vocab-def">Write eval test cases first, then build the feature. TDD for AI.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Faithfulness</div><div class="vocab-def">RAG metric: does the answer only use info from retrieved docs? No hallucinated additions.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">FID (Frechet Inception Distance)</div><div class="vocab-def">Image quality metric: compares distribution of generated images to real images. Lower = better.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Ground Truth</div><div class="vocab-def">The known-correct answer for a test case. The answer key your eval checks against.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Hallucination</div><div class="vocab-def">When the AI confidently states something that isn't true. The #1 concern in AI product quality.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Inter-Rater Agreement</div><div class="vocab-def">How much different judges (human or LLM) agree. Measured by Cohen's Kappa or correlation.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">LLM-as-Judge</div><div class="vocab-def">Using an LLM to grade another LLM's output. Scalable but needs calibration against human judgment.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Meta-Eval</div><div class="vocab-def">Evaluating your eval system itself. "Does my LLM judge agree with human experts?"</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">MOS (Mean Opinion Score)</div><div class="vocab-def">Audio quality metric from human raters. Scale 1&ndash;5. Used for voice AI evaluation.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">N-shot</div><div class="vocab-def">How many examples shown to the model. Zero-shot = none. Few-shot = 2&ndash;5 examples. More shots usually = better quality.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">nDCG</div><div class="vocab-def">Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain. Search/recommendation metric: are the best results ranked highest?</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">NLI (Natural Language Inference)</div><div class="vocab-def">Does statement A entail, contradict, or is neutral to statement B? Used in hallucination detection.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Open Coding</div><div class="vocab-def">First pass of error analysis: label each failure with a description. No predefined categories.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">pass@k</div><div class="vocab-def">Probability of at least 1 success in k attempts. Use when one success is enough (code gen).</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">pass^k</div><div class="vocab-def">Probability of ALL k attempts succeeding. Use when consistency matters (customer support).</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Precision@K</div><div class="vocab-def">Of the top K results shown, how many are relevant? Search/recommendation metric.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">RAG</div><div class="vocab-def">Retrieval-Augmented Generation: search for docs first, then generate answer using those docs. 70% of enterprise AI uses this.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Recall@K</div><div class="vocab-def">Of all relevant items that exist, how many appear in the top K? Search metric.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Regression</div><div class="vocab-def">Quality got worse after a change. The eval equivalent of "you broke it."</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">ROUGE</div><div class="vocab-def">Text overlap metric for summarization. Measures word overlap between generated and reference summary.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Rubric</div><div class="vocab-def">Criteria for judging AI output. Defines dimensions, score levels, and examples for each level.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">SEM (Standard Error of Mean)</div><div class="vocab-def">How much your average score would vary if you re-ran the eval. Smaller = more reliable.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">System Prompt</div><div class="vocab-def">Hidden instructions to the AI that the user never sees. Defines behavior, rules, persona. The PM's main lever.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Temperature</div><div class="vocab-def">Controls randomness (0&ndash;1). Low = deterministic. High = creative/variable. Affects eval reproducibility.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Token</div><div class="vocab-def">The unit LLMs process text in. ~4 characters or ~3/4 of a word. Pricing and limits are in tokens.</div></div>
  <div class="vocab-card"><div class="vocab-term">Win Rate</div><div class="vocab-def">In A/B comparison, how often does version A beat version B? &gt;60% on 40+ cases is usually significant.</div></div>
</div>

<div style="text-align: center; padding: 64px 0 0; color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 13px;">
  <div style="width: 40px; height: 2px; background: var(--coral); margin: 0 auto 16px;"></div>
  Built for PMs who vibecode &mdash; grounded in research from Anthropic, OpenAI, Hamel Husain, and 40+ sources.<br>
  &copy; 2026 &middot; AI PM Eval Masterclass v2
</div>

</div></div>

<button class="back-to-top" id="backToTop" onclick="window.scrollTo({top:0})">&#8593;</button>

<script>
const pf=document.getElementById('progressFill'),bt=document.getElementById('backToTop');
const nl=document.querySelectorAll('.nav-link'),secs=[];
nl.forEach(l=>{const id=l.getAttribute('href')?.slice(1);if(id){const el=document.getElementById(id);if(el)secs.push({id,el,link:l})}});
let t=false;
window.addEventListener('scroll',()=>{if(!t){requestAnimationFrame(()=>{
  const s=window.scrollY,d=document.documentElement.scrollHeight-window.innerHeight;
  pf.style.width=(d>0?(s/d)*100:0)+'%';
  bt.classList.toggle('visible',s>600);
  let c=secs[0];for(const x of secs)if(x.el.offsetTop<=s+120)c=x;
  nl.forEach(l=>l.classList.remove('active'));if(c)c.link.classList.add('active');
  t=false;});t=true;}});
nl.forEach(l=>l.addEventListener('click',()=>{if(window.innerWidth<=960)document.getElementById('sidebar').classList.remove('open')}));
</script>
</body>
</html>
